america's first newspaper started  publishing long before the nation had  even been born  the first was printed in boston starting  in 1690.  but in the centuries since we've come to  rely on the daily edition of our  favorite paper  in one entire u.s state though that  tradition is about to come  to an end major garrett has the story  for more than 100 years the casper star  tribune has served the state of wyoming  john bailey a doctor in casper has  subscribed for  decades we've taken a paper for probably  55 or 60 years  and i've always read the paper as a way  i think to get  local news the news that's important  within the community which  may be the most important news there is  and they've walked with our community  through the ups and the downs and the  booms and the bus  brooke kaufman who once worked for the  paper heads  visit casper and the convention bureau  so i feel like having them as a partner  and somebody that's really foundational  in our community  has made a difference and it is critical  and i think it's a news outlet that  keeps us informed about what's going on  in our backyard  starting this month the star tribune  will cut the number of days it prints  the paper from seven to five  this continues a downsizing trend in  2008 the paper had 40 reporters and six  bureaus now it has 13 reporters and  one bureau wyoming is a big place and  the paper is receding from towns like  lost springs population four  brad reese farms there so out here  in rural wyoming you still like  traditions you still like sitting down  with your family to meals  and so that tradition of a newspaper  coming that you can sit down and open  i think that's something that we're  going to miss it kind of hurts to lose  that  the monday and tuesday editions will  only be available  online and when that happens wyoming  will become the only state in the nation  without a daily  printed newspaper for brad reese  holding a newspaper is different and  better  i'm tired of getting on the internet you  know what i mean i'm tired of computers  i don't want to look at a computer  anymore  i just want to live my life and go enjoy  it and sometimes that just means sitting  down with a nice drink and a  newspaper to read you know one of my  first thoughts when i heard the news was  you know how many of our older readers  are suddenly gonna you know go out  spend 400 on an ipad to read the e  edition  nick reynolds is the star tribune's  state house reporter  you know we adapt to things all the time  but if we just think about how long  print has been around and how  steady of a presence it's been i mean  we're talking centuries here and you  know this is like the greatest  disruption to one of the most consistent  mediums  um in human history penelope abernathy  researches the decline of newspapers and  the rise of digital media at the  university of north carolina school of  journalism  cutting back on the printing and  distribution of a newspaper on certain  days makes a lot of economic sense  for newspapers what it often doesn't  take into account  is the dependence that people in  outlying areas  that do not have access to digital  alternatives  wyoming ranks near the bottom nationally  in internet access  meaning on days it does not print a  paper the star tribune's local and  regional news coverage will be invisible  in one fifth of the state and what does  a local community lose  when this type of community journalism  disappears  you typically lose the person who shows  up at the school board meeting  when you have a pullback in both  coverage and circulation  of a state and regional paper what you  typically lose is coverage of the major  issues in both the region and the state  we depend on the state and regional  newspapers  to kind of bring it all together for us  and help us understand what the truly  important issues are in wyoming a  six-hour commute  is not uncommon knitting the state  together is a role the star tribune  played  in print every single day now some news  there  as it has been in other parts of rural  america  will fall through the cracks in the  internet  for cbs this morning saturday major  garrett  washington because of poor internet  access so a fifth of readers then  wouldn't have access to that and i know  it's a silly thing but yeah for some  people it is  that's access but the silly thing to me  is sometimes you do just want to hold  that paper  yeah you heard one of the men in there  all right  have a drink read the paper yeah exactly  all right
 

